              "a11yRetry": "Retry the task. Reset all responses and start the task over again.",
              "textField": "Both of the medications that are most commonly used to treat growth hormone excesses or deficiencies need to be injected, subcutaneously or intramuscularly. *Somatropin* is given for growth hormone deficiencies, as it is a synthetic form of growth hormone. *Lanreotide* inhibits the secretion of growth hormone, and as such is used, although the person with growth hormone excess will normally need to have the pituitary adenoma causing the problem removed. The term for an excess in growth hormone after the person has stopped growing is *acromegaly*, the term for excesses in childhood is called *gigantism*."
            },
            "library": "H5P.DragText 1.10",
            "metadata": {
              "contentType": "Drag the Words",
              "license": "U",
              "title": "Untitled Drag the Words"
            },
            "subContentId": "404fb868-ff97-46c7-be80-1dcff4e3f61e"
          },
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The use of somatropin for off-label uses like gaining muscle will result in an increase of all internal organs except the brain</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The use of somatropin for off-label uses like gaining muscle will result in an increase in glucose levels and an increased risk of developing diabetes mellitus type 2</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Growth hormone deficiency in childhood results in failure to thrive and is most commonly idiopathic, meaning that there is no identifiable cause but it is thought that many of those cases are due to the child not having adequate or undisturbed sleep</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, this one is incorrect. GH is released from the anterior pituitary gland</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>As you know, GH is released from the anterior pituitary gland</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Growth hormone is released from the posterior pituitary gland.</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>The largest diurnal surge of growth hormone is released as a person goes to sleep, and for a few hours after.</div>\n"
                }
              ],
